ntiable on  $(-5,4).$  What is the missing part of the sentence in the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,4)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on  $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following: \n\n*There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-5$ and $b=4.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-5)}{4-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-2}{4+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,4)$ such that ${f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{9}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-73349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/veri