f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/b246b2835d856e4ec43bd9a96275ddf5531b7d653d46e5c58ae9553b6b59956e.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function  $g(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(1,4).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Hence, $h(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a cusp at $x=2.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-483