63/assets/b629cd9686bb3f9b671a9412552ccd4161cb832d8e8426ee83a8955b81cc5831.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/ec5215bf37cce6a5b6a6f6cddcc6366e757ae464bdbde83c7276ca2db85d64cd.png"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/ec5215bf37cce6a5b6a6f6cddcc6366e757ae464bdbde83c7276ca2db85d64cd.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)=2|x|$\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $h(x)=|x+1|$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $f(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[0,4]$ but has a corner at $x=1.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{4}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ is not defined on $(-\\infty ,1).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function II satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3a41212c7a38beab938478a00354b0b6afd96caed552d8eac50ca7b498a56b85" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c32