cation.json"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/state.json" "file_sha256" "c8df4a86e095daad9a95b1e58d910b1c9f43e98bc9b2f2c326fcdeda5f40dc2f" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\, .$ *\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/5672cc68e55078623f34f01650d3e82bd3cee62e6ca97fabcd2287f2a865501a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3718.json" "file_sha256" "b848f56c46eca570a8494b5a1f06ca9c9c21e811716f35b79eb4ff20a4a400d4" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b),$ the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n$f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.$*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4,$ so the above statement becomes \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-1)}{4-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2}{4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3718.json" "file_sha256" "b848f56c46eca570a8494b5a1f06ca9c9c21e811716f35b79eb4ff20a4a400d4" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3717.json" "file_sha256" "9c02721c0969f0299723ac1dce6b7790f9a64332ef10c68c8c6c2cb8e6933c64" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$  is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$ \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that \n\n$$\nf'(c)   = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3717.json" "file_sha256" "9c02721c0969f0299723ac1dce6b7790f9a64332ef10c68c8c6c2cb8e6933c64" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[−2,2]?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/e680c2e80874a4c2e8b4d31fcc7a6ba42a3c55fb7c6f559e42f3cd785b22e3ed.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3719.json" "file_sha256" "85619a0b932b3fdecf155ade34ce6e96a9d1eb8014375f28521dab320577d45d" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-3719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2).$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2.$ So it is not continuous on $[-2,2],$ and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2,$ it is right-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[−2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0.$ So, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(−2,2).$  Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-3719.json" "file_sha256" "85619a0b932b3fdecf155ade34ce6e96a9d1eb8014375f28521dab320577d45d" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n\n- $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n\n- $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-6738.json" "file_sha256" "6f11550634d3d4dd82ad314aa8b38472afd2df84c81c8b3dbb07050a0ad37375" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x = 0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[0,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/example-6738.json" "file_sha256" "6f11550634d3d4dd82ad314aa8b38472afd2df84c81c8b3dbb07050a0ad37375" "basis" 1532 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "4b8092cfde0d32ce65370dcd601054546f470d85048934180c21baa7f52efc70" "basis" 1866 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $m(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $n(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $n(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $l(x)$  has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $l(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$  has removable discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "4b8092cfde0d32ce65370dcd601054546f470d85048934180c21baa7f52efc70" "basis" 1866 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "374bb6edf5e57c21aa77f6699efe016d87b5b97e3e537b225b290f16d2251808" "basis" 1866 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058243/state.json" "file_sha256" "4b8092cfde0d32ce65370dcd601054546f470d85048934180c21baa7f52efc70" "basis" 1866 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/ref