te.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-125237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(c)=5"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=-2"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=0"] ["math" "{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{f}^{′}(c)=-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-345063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x)=5\\sqrt{x}-2$ on the interval $[4,9].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-345063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[4,9]$ and differentiable on $(4,9),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (4,9)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=4$ and $b=9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(9)-f(4)}{9-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5\\sqrt{9}-2)-(5\\sqrt{4}-2)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{13-8}{5} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5\\sqrt{x}-2)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{c}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ c & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-345063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-345063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-345063" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{25}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡x$ on the interval $[4,16].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[4,16]$ and differentiable on $(4,16),$ hence, we can apply the Mean Value Theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (4,16)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=4$ and $b=16$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(16)-f(4)}{16-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(16)-{log}_{2}⁡(4)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-2}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{2}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ c & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-125251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] ["math" "{log}_{2}⁡6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-345316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)={x}^{2}+1$ on the interval $[-1,3].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-345316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,3]$ and differentiable on $(-1,3),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem.  \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (-1,3)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=-1$ and $b=3$ into the above gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(3)-f(-1)}{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{({3}^{2}+1)-((-1)^{2}+1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-2}{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+1)=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-345316" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-345316" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-345316" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x)={log}_{3}⁡x$ on the interval $[9,81].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[9,81]$ and differentiable on $(9,81),$ hence, we can apply the Mean Value Theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (9,81)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=9$ and $b=81$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(81)-f(9)}{81-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(81)-{log}_{3}⁡(9)}{72} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-2}{72} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{3}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ c & =\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-125252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{18}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}"] ["math" "\\frac{36}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}"] ["math" "\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}"] ["math" "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}"] ["math" "\\frac{72}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125252" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-2317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)={x}^{3}$ on the interval $[0,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-2317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (0,1)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(1)-f(0)}{1-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})=3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=3{c}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{c}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {c}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ c & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c\\in (0,1)$, we discard the negative value, therefore $c=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-2317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-2317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-2317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-2317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function  $g(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$   Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(1,4).$  Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$  has a vertical asymptote at $x=1$ and is not defined for $x\\in (-\\infty ,1].$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=3.$ Hence, $h(x)$  is not continuous on $[-1,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)$  is continuous on $[-1,4]$ but has a cusp at $x=2.$  Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=2,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48396" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/0680d1da0bbcc0d40668bef248d55441acd77c1304545d6710256eb023023b05.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/6283b99ea5ee48cf8dbbf56297a03d29e7de3a09ed3fdeac9376b3a875d045d6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/6cd605b5533bf0211bf75d3cc1d461fe41ee382e8799bd2337c4a53627eb0ca9.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/b629cd9686bb3f9b671a9412552ccd4161cb832d8e8426ee83a8955b81cc5831.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/ec5215bf37cce6a5b6a6f6