multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $m(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $n(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $n(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $l(x)$  has jump discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $l(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$  has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $q(x)$  has removable discontinuities at $x=0,2.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,2].$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/787772f6d68af4df373cbfdd6b7a3cf3d313db7e03ecce36437f01068b738408.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/8d37dc99a006ba89bc6a874d251a065fc714b260072740206f9cc3692b08c604.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/d5927b5cc43ee1b5923b4d2aa4b4740e2bbc1c73b09b00525cb74767badc4808.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/f5d7744d62f94b96b8bd1600ca7f5fc752d1f83c8c8928e740261e808cb9e07b.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-127011" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/4c9f5d5245a1a4aa33a0d474ff328a30a1c8820055551e3613bf7c9489c2a908.png"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $g(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ but has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, $h(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$ has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1.$ Hence, $s(x)$ is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/09017ce160ebb2995e0c7c4efe81fd5444ba8cedd91dc270c69757b8b0eb1ae4.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/45db5f9f600f29e91fb40335f80fb28bb12f87fc4618c623354b91408f6bb09c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/b246b2835d856e4ec43bd9a96275ddf5531b7d653d46e5c58ae9553b6b59956e.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/c4c45b3d88dd9821c1c5650e898d806b8887838c609548296040feae8915e40b.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/e50696609559e7996dda87b3566639e0f1772b0538b9b41e8311cb49b5c41a5b.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125317" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/b246b2835d856e4ec43bd9a96275ddf5531b7d653d46e5c58ae9553b6b59956e.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)=2|x|$\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $h(x)=|x+1|$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=2|x|$ is continuous on $[-1,1],$ but it has a corner in $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=1-x$ over the given interval. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $h(x)=|x+1|$ is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1)$ because it is equivalent to $p(x)=x+1$ over the given interval. Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the graph of $y=h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ but this point does not lie inside $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, only functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-127013" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3]?$\n\n- $g(x)={log}_{2}⁡x$\n- $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x-2}$\n- $h(x)=x-2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)={log}_{2}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x-2}$ is not defined at $x=2$, so it is not continuous at $x=2$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,3].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x-2$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-48500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48500" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)={e}^{x}$\n- $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$\n- $h(x)=x|x|$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{x}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=x|x|$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125259" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-125259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-125259" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4]?$\n\n- $g(x)=|x-1|$\n- $f(x)={x}^{4}$\n- $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|x-1|$ is continuous on $[0,4]$ but has a corner at $x=1.$ Hence, it is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{4}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ is not defined on $(-\\infty ,1).$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[0,4].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only function II satisfies the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-48497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-77864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]?$\n\n- $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$\n- $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$\n- $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-77864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=|{x}^{2}+1|$ is equivalent to $p(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ so it is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, the functions I, II, and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-77864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f390378c1221a74f9572acd1218f2dc69830ecf8932ab444c1ccc83ee35204d2"} {"question" "q-77864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-77864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "q-77864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/state.json" "file_sha256" "8f716d7354f66ba6857898b997d23a4d7cd2bed7f6a5f768d2ef0e67da15e37c"} {"question" "e-3718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\, .$ *\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/5672cc68e55078623f34f01650d3e82bd3cee62e6ca97fabcd2287f2a865501a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/example-3718.json" "file_sha256" "082c034ee7114ced2f11184357a0433794ee088e5b8a326478694221e4439d1f"} {"question" "e-3718" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b),$ the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n$f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.$*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4,$ so the above statement becomes \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-1)}{4-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2}{4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/example-3718.json" "file_sha256" "082c034ee7114ced2f11184357a0433794ee088e5b8a326478694221e4439d1f"} {"question" "e-3717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/example-3717.json" "file_sha256" "c0de1648cb213d98cdebc5245881d1f63e301bd10ebaeded4a64a11ab2de3d60"} {"question" "e-3717" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $f(x)$  is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$ \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that \n\n$$\nf'(c)   = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/example-3717.json" "file_sha256" "c0de1648cb213d98cdebc5245881d1f63e301bd10ebaeded4a64a11ab2de3d60"} {"question" "e-3719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[−2,2]?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/assets/e680c2e80874a4c2e8b4d31fcc7a6ba42a3c55fb7c6f559e42f3cd785b22e3ed.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/example-3719.json" "file_sha256" "4bd7fa221f182b8f908b383c9095a675a48b44c554b37d3c36a2aa71580c04f1"} {"question" "e-3719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2).$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2.$ So it is not continuous on $[-2,2],$ and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2,$ it is right-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[−2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0.$ So, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(−2,2).$  Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/example-3719.json" "file_sha256" "4bd7fa221f182b8f908b383c9095a675a48b44c554b37d3c36a2aa71580c04f1"} {"question" "e-6738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]?$\n\n- $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n\n- $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n\n- $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/example-6738.json" "file_sha256" "f593a88921c8c4b0504f904442a1b6297fa7ecc7fbfb6db553ab8f4c0bb29676"} {"question" "e-6738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x = 0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[0,1].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074895/example-6738.json" "file_sha256" "f593a88921c8c4b0504f904442a1b6297fa7ecc7fbfb6db553ab8f4c0bb29676"}] "usage" {"q-48396" [] "q-48554" [] "q-127013" [] "q-125237" [] "q-345063" [] "q-48497" [] "e-3719" [] "q-127011" [] "q-73349" [] "e-6738" [] "e-3717" [] "q-73354" [] "q-125317" [] "e-3718" [] "q-77888" []} "basis" 2743 "retractions" [[17592186314224 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/6393810d90b8d340dbde041c43cb7e146cb59fedfcfd4a678827d291ea502b00.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,4]$ and differentiable on  $(-5,4).$  What is the missing part of the sentence in the statement below?\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,4)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *"] [17592186314231 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041963/assets/029a51dccba9f1ec927c820fb8adb72e926b88a881f4646090ac651ef29da271.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[-5,3]$ and differentiable on $(-5,3).$ Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-5,3)$ such that $\\text{_______}\\,.$ *"] [17592186064740 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/288/Source/Images/q-48554.png)\n\nThe function $y = f(x)$ above is continuous on the interval $[- 2, 4]$ and differentiable on $(-2, 4)$. Complete the sentence below.\n\n> *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c ∈ (-2, 4)$ such that $\\mid ___